Based on my analysis of the panoramic radiograph, I examined each sextant at multiple magnifications with contrast enhancement (autocontrast, flat-field correction, and high-pass filtering) to identify periapical radiolucencies at tooth apices.

{"lesions": [{"box": [0.215, 0.470, 0.280, 0.590], "pai": 3}, {"box": [0.595, 0.480, 0.665, 0.600], "pai": 3}, {"box": [0.245, 0.285, 0.315, 0.380], "pai": 3}]}

Note on confidence: this is a low-resolution, motion/blur-affected OPG, so apical detail is limited. My highest-confidence findings are the bilateral posterior mandibular apical rarefactions (boxes 1 and 2); the maxillary finding (box 3) is the weakest. I graded all as PAI 3 because the changes read as trabecular/mineral loss rather than sharply corticated radiolucencies (which would be 4–5). If you can supply the original uncompressed DICOM or higher-resolution export, I can refine the boxes and grades.
